        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "I actually use `autocmd VimResized * wincmd =`, and I find the default `equalalways` goes well together with it. But it always felt like a limiting compromise because only equal distributions of window widths/heights are well supported. A 75%-25% split will become 50%-50% after resize. The inability to\n> temporarily resize the container terminal/GUI without messing up your window layout\n\nis definitely what annoys me the most. It also feels glitchy that the splitters can potentially make big jumps into the equalized positions when the terminal is only resized by one cell row. So, I think it's nice that it's easily configurable and documented, but it also seems a bit unpolished for a default.\n\nFor me, the only real improvement would be if all proportions would be preserved by default, which doesn't necessarily need fractional splits if the current proportions are calculated on resize. If I understand correctly, [vim-ProportionalResize](https://github.com/inkarkat/vim-ProportionalResize) does this by proactively storing the current terminal size somewhere because a `VimResized` handler doesn't automatically have access to the previous size.",
            "created_at": "2026-07-31T11:17:09Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/41059#issuecomment-5142276405",
            "id": 5142276405,
